# =============================================================================
#  TypR tutor  ·  Lesson 11 of 12  ·  Talk to R
# =============================================================================
#
#  Every R function and every R package stays available from TypR. But the
#  compiler cannot guess the types of a function it has never seen: what it
#  returns is 'any', a value it knows nothing about. You can use an 'any'
#  freely, but you cannot pretend it is a char.
#
#  To type an R function, declare its SIGNATURE with '@':
#
#      @nchar: (char) -> int;
#
#  This generates no code at all; it only teaches the compiler. From then
#  on, every call to nchar is checked like one of your own functions.
#
#  TypR's standard library already declares many base R functions this way.
#  For the rest (base R or any package), you write the '@' line yourself.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  >>> EXERCISE
#
#  R's strrep(x, times) repeats the string x 'times' times:
#  strrep("-", 3) gives "---". This program does not compile, because
#  strrep returns 'any' and 'line' must be a char.
#
#  Add a signature for strrep ABOVE its first use, so that it compiles.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

let line: char <- strrep("-", 20);

print(line);
